      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Use of contrast improves detection of systolic regional wall motion abnormalities (RWMAs) during stress echocardiography. We evaluated regional diastolic contour abnormalities (RDCAs) that were associated with coronary artery disease (CAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "From August of 2003 to September of 2004, we evaluated 89 patients who underwent contrast stress echocardiography (CSE) and coronary angiography within a 3-month period (\"invasive\" group) and 17 patients with lower CAD risk who underwent CSE only (\"reference\" group)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "RDCAs were present in 73 patients in the invasive group and were associated with higher Framingham risk scores (relative risk, 3.6; 95% confidence interval, 1.9-6.6). RDCAs were present in 1 patient in the reference group. When combined with RWMA, RDCA improved sensitivity of CSE from 78% to 97% and specificity from 26% to 59% (diagnostic threshold for CAD was 70% stenosis)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "RDCAs were a novel observation associated with higher CAD risk and improved the diagnostic accuracy of CSE."
        }
      ]},
